  "query_preview": "..\nTuesday night through Wednesday night:The Pacific Northwest will \nremain under strong westerly flow through early Wednesday morning, \ndriven by a 120+ kt jet streak over southern British Columbia. As \nthe jet sags slightly southward tonight, 250 mb winds across \nnorthern Washington and the Idaho Panhandle will increase to 100+ \nkts, sustaining gusty winds along mountain ridges through the night. \n Meanwhile, drier air filters into the northern valleys tonight into \nWednesday morning clearing clouds for much of Wednesday. The jet \nbegins to slowly lift northward and weaken Wednesday night into \nThursday with moisture also migrating back northward ahead of a weak \nweather system approaching the Pacific Northwest. Before the \nmoisture lifts northward, clear skies and light winds early \nWednesday night will allow low temperatures across the northern \nvalleys to plummet into the 30s. Uncertainty exists in how quickly \nthe temperatures can decrease before cloud cover increases again \novernight, which will limit the radiative cooling. Currently, the \nNational Blend of Models gives Deer Park 20% chance for temperatures \nbelow 36F. \n\nThursday through Monday: A warming trend will develop heading into \nthe weekend as an upper-level ridge builds across the western US. \nFlat zonal flow will persist initially over the Inland Northwest \nThursday and Friday with weak weather systems moving through \nThursday and Friday. Outside of a small chance (20%) for light rain \nshowers across the north Cascades both days, these systems will \nprimarily result in increased cloud cover for the rest of the Inland \nNorthwest.  Ensembles are in good agreement for the ridge to become \nestablished over the Pacific Northwest this weekend, initiating a \nprolonged period of dry weather and above normal temperatures. \nBeyond Monday, ensemble consensus favors persistent upper-level \nridging remaining in place through the first week of October. \nHowever, 25% of ensembles show the ridge to be weak enough to allow \nfor weather systems to flatten it by the middle of next week, but \nthis is an unlikely scenario at this point. /vmt",
